.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt MOUNT_EXFAT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount_exfat
.Nd mount an exFAT file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl g Ar gid
.Op Fl M Ar mask
.Op Fl m Ar mask
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Op Fl u Ar uid
.Ar special node
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility attaches the exFAT file system residing on
the device
.Pa special
to the global file system namespace at the location
indicated by
.Pa node .
This command is normally executed by
.Xr mount 8
at boot time or manually to mount an exFAT volume.
.Pp
Prior to mounting, the driver computes and validates the 32-bit Boot Region
Checksum across the boot sub-regions.
If the Main Boot Region fails checksum or structure validation, the driver
automatically falls back to the Backup Boot Region and forces a read-only
mount with the volume dirty flag set.
If both the Main and Backup Boot Regions are corrupted, the mount operation
fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
Transactional exFAT
.Pq TexFAT
volumes formatted with two File Allocation Tables
.Pq Li NumberOfFATs = 2
are automatically mounted read-only to preserve filesystem consistency.
Attempts to remount such volumes read-write using
.Xr mount 8
.Pq Fl u Cm rw
are rejected with
.Er EROFS .
.Pp
Per section 3.1.13.1 of the exFAT specification, volumes formatted with a single
File Allocation Table
.Pq Li NumberOfFATs = 1
must have
.Va ActiveFat
cleared in
.Va VolumeFlags .
If
.Va ActiveFat
is set on a single-FAT volume, the mount operation fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
Per section 3.1.13.4 of the exFAT specification, volumes with
.Va ClearToZero
set in
.Va VolumeFlags
require reformatting before mounting and are rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
Per section 6.2.1 of the exFAT specification, file system operations reject
unrecognized critical primary directory entries (entry type codes 0x80 through
0x9F) with
.Er EINVAL .
Benign primary directory entries (type codes 0xA0 through 0xBF), such as
Volume GUID and TexFAT Padding entries, are safely ignored during lookups.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl g Ar gid
Set the group of the files in the file system to
.Ar gid .
The argument may be a group name or a numeric group ID.
The default group is the group of the directory
on which the file system is being mounted.
.It Fl M Ar mask
Specify the maximum file permissions for directories
in the file system.
The value is specified in octal (e.g., 755 or 0755).
If
.Fl M
is specified without
.Fl m ,
the file permission mask defaults to the directory mask.
If neither is specified, the default directory mask is inherited from the mount
point.
.It Fl m Ar mask
Specify the maximum file permissions for regular files
in the file system.
The value is specified in octal (e.g., 755 or 0755).
If
.Fl m
is specified without
.Fl M ,
the directory permission mask defaults to the file mask.
If neither is specified, the default file mask is inherited from the mount
point.
.It Fl o Ar options
Use the specified mount options, as described in
.Xr mount 8 .
Multiple options may be specified as a comma-separated list.
The following file-system-specific options are available:
.Bl -tag -width "dirmask=mask"
.It Cm dirmask Ns = Ns Ar mask
Equivalent to
.Fl M .
.It Cm gid Ns = Ns Ar gid
Equivalent to
.Fl g .
.It Cm mask Ns = Ns Ar mask
Equivalent to
.Fl m .
.It Cm notrim
Do not issue TRIM commands when clusters are freed (the default).
.It Cm ro
Mount the file system read-only.
Equivalent to
.Fl r .
.It Cm rw
Mount the file system read-write (the default).
Equivalent to
.Fl w .
.It Cm trim
Issue BIO_DELETE (TRIM/discard) commands to the underlying device
when clusters are freed.
.It Cm uid Ns = Ns Ar uid
Equivalent to
.Fl u .
.It Cm update
Equivalent to the
.Fl u
argument to
.Xr mount 8 .
Allows updating mount options (such as toggling read-only or read-write status)
on an already mounted file system.
.El
.It Fl r
Mount the file system read-only.
This is equivalent to the
.Cm ro
argument to the
.Fl o
option.
.It Fl u Ar uid
Set the owner of the files in the file system to
.Ar uid .
The argument may be a user name or a numeric user ID.
The default owner is the owner of the directory
on which the file system is being mounted.
.It Fl w
Mount the file system read-write (the default).
This is equivalent to the
.Cm rw
argument to the
.Fl o
option.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
To mount an exFAT volume located on
.Pa /dev/da0s1
onto
.Pa /mnt :
.Pp
.Dl "mount_exfat /dev/da0s1 /mnt"
.Pp
To mount an exFAT volume read-only:
.Pp
.Dl "mount_exfat -r /dev/da0s1 /mnt"
.Pp
To mount with specific user and permissions:
.Pp
.Dl "mount_exfat -u 1001 -g 1001 -m 755 -M 755 /dev/da0s1 /mnt"
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nmount 2 ,
.Xr exfat 4 ,
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr fsck_exfat 8 ,
.Xr glabel 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr newfs_exfat 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Fx 15.1 .
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
utility was developed for the
.Fx
Project.
